  "prediction": "The report discusses the issue of neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS) in newborns caused by their mothers' use of opioids during pregnancy. The report highlights that the number of NAS cases has been increasing in the United States, leading to significant health and economic costs. The report provides an overview of the causes, symptoms, and diagnosis of NAS, as well as current treatment methods.\n\nThe report also discusses the efforts being made to address the issue, including state and federal initiatives to expand access to substance use treatment for pregnant women and new mothers. The report highlights the importance of early identification and intervention for NAS, as well as the need for continued research to improve treatment methods and prevent NAS.\n\nThe report also touches upon the economic costs of NAS, including the high cost of hospitalization and long-term care for affected infants. The report calls for increased funding and resources to address the issue and support families affected by opioid use during pregnancy.\n\nOverall, the report emphasizes the need for a comprehensive approach to address the issue of NAS, including expanding access to substance use treatment, improving early identification and intervention, and addressing the economic costs of the condition.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the issue of neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS) in newborns caused by their mothers' use of opioids during pregnancy. The report highlights that the number of NAS cases has been increasing in the United States, leading to significant health and economic costs. The report provides an overview of the causes, symptoms, and diagnosis of NAS, as well as current treatment methods.\n\nThe report also discusses the efforts being made to address the issue, including state and federal initiatives to expand access to substance use treatment for pregnant women and new mothers. The report highlights the importance of early identification and intervention for NAS, as well as the need for continued research to improve treatment methods and prevent NAS.\n\nThe report also touches upon the economic costs of NAS, including the high cost of hospitalization and long-term care for affected infants. The report calls for increased funding and resources to address the issue and support families affected by opioid use during pregnancy.\n\nOverall, the report emphasizes the need for a comprehensive approach to address the issue of NAS, including expanding access to substance use treatment, improving early identification and intervention, and addressing the economic costs of the condition.",
